                    "title": "Series RLC Resonance and Bandwidth",
                    "imageUrl": "assets/images/series_rlc_resonance_bandwidth.jpg",
                    "imageTitle": "Series RLC Resonance Peak Half-Power Bandwidth Frequency Response Blueprint",
                    "formula": "$$\\omega_0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{L C}}, \\quad Q_{\\text{factor}} = \\frac{\\omega_0 L}{R}, \\quad BW = \\frac{\\omega_0}{Q_{\\text{factor}}} = \\frac{R}{L}$$",
                    "description": "At resonant frequency $\\omega_0$, inductive and capacitive reactances cancel ($X_L = X_C$), minimizing circuit impedance to pure resistance $R$.",
                    "examTip": "Found in Frequency Response. Resonant frequency in Hertz is $f_0 = \\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{LC}}$. Half-power cutoff frequencies are $\\omega_{1,2} = \\omega_0 \\pm BW / 2$.",
                    "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Series_RLC_Resonance_and_Bandwidth.mp4",
                    "videoTitle": "Series RLC Resonance and Bandwidth Explainer",
                    "videoDuration": "10s",
                    "solvedExample": {
                        "question": "<p>A series RLC resonant circuit has resistance $R = 10\\ \\Omega$, inductance $L = 50\\text{ mH}$ ($0.05\\text{ H}$), and capacitance $C = 2.0\\ \\mu\\text{F}$ ($2.0 \\times 10^{-6}\\text{ F}$).</p>\n<p>The resonant angular frequency $\\omega_0$ and the circuit bandwidth $BW$ (in rad/s) are most nearly:</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $\\omega_0 = 1,000\\text{ rad/s},\\quad BW = 100\\text{ rad/s}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $\\omega_0 = 3,162\\text{ rad/s},\\quad BW = 200\\text{ rad/s}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $\\omega_0 = 3,162\\text{ rad/s},\\quad BW = 500\\text{ rad/s}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $\\omega_0 = 10,000\\text{ rad/s},\\quad BW = 200\\text{ rad/s}$</div>\n</div>",
                        "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Calculate Resonant Angular Frequency ($\\omega_0$)</strong></p>\n$$\\omega_0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{L C}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{0.05 \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-6}}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10^{-7}}} = \\frac{1}{3.1623 \\times 10^{-4}} \\approx 3,162\\text{ rad/s}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Calculate Bandwidth ($BW$)</strong></p>\n$$BW = \\frac{R}{L} = \\frac{10\\ \\Omega}{0.05\\text{ H}} = 200\\text{ rad/s}$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 3: Circuit Quality Factor ($Q$) Check</strong></p>\n$$Q = \\frac{\\omega_0}{BW} = \\frac{3,162}{200} \\approx 15.8$$\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(B) $\\omega_0 = 3,162\\text{ rad/s},\\quad BW = 200\\text{ rad/s}$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> At resonance in a series RLC circuit, inductive and capacitive reactances cancel ($X_L = X_C$), minimizing impedance to pure resistance $R$.</p>"
                    }
