                    "title": "Thermoplastics vs. Thermosetting Plastics",
                    "formula": "$$\\text{Thermoplastics: Linear/branched polymers (Van der Waals secondary bonds)}, \\quad T > T_g / T_m \\implies \\text{Reversibly Meltable / Recyclable};$$ $$\\text{Thermosets: Heavily cross-linked 3D covalent network}, \\quad \\Delta H_{\\text{cure}} \\implies \\text{Decomposes/Chars on heating (Non-meltable)}$$",
                    "description": "Thermoplastics (PE, PP, PVC, PS, PTFE, Nylon, PET) comprise linear or branched macromolecular chains bound by weak secondary intermolecular forces (Van der Waals/hydrogen bonds). They reversibly soften and melt upon heating and solidify upon cooling, enabling repeated remolding, extrusion, and recycling. Thermosets (Epoxy, Phenolic, Polyurethane, Bakelite, Vulcanized Rubber) undergo irreversible chemical cross-linking during curing, creating an interconnected 3D covalent network that will not melt when reheated; excessive heat causes thermal degradation and charring.",
                    "examTip": "Found in Materials Science: Polymers. Thermoplastics = linear/branched, meltable, recyclable, high ductility above $T_g$. Thermosets = 3D cross-linked covalent network, non-meltable, higher thermal stability and stiffness, brittle fracture. Elastomers (like rubber) have light cross-linking providing high elastic recovery.",
                    "isNew": true,
                    "videoUrl": "assets/videos/Thermoplastics_vs_Thermosetting_Plastics.mp4",
                    "videoTitle": "Thermoplastics vs. Thermosetting Plastics Explainer",
                    "videoDuration": "10s",
                    "solvedExample": {
                        "question": "<p>Which of the following polymeric materials is classified as a <em>thermosetting plastic</em> that undergoes irreversible cross-linking and degrades/chars rather than melting when reheated?</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) Polyethylene (PE)</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) Polypropylene (PP)</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) Epoxy resin</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)</div>\n</div>",
                        "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Classify Polymer Molecular Architecture</strong></p>\n<p>Polyethylene (PE), Polypropylene (PP), and Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) consist of linear or branched polymer chains held by weak secondary Van der Waals bonds, making them <strong>thermoplastics</strong> that melt reversibly when heated.</p>\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Identify Thermoset Cross-Linking</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Epoxy resin</strong> forms a rigid 3D network of permanent covalent cross-links during curing. When reheated, these covalent bonds do not melt; continued heating causes thermal decomposition (charring).</p>\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(C) Epoxy resin</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> Thermoplastics = PE, PP, PVC, PS, Nylon, PTFE, PET (meltable, recyclable). Thermosets = Epoxy, Phenolic (Bakelite), Polyurethane, Vulcanized Rubber (non-meltable, char on heating).</p>"
                    }
