                    "description": "Hardness is a material's resistance to localized permanent surface plastic deformation (indentation, scratching, or abrasion). Primary standardized indentation tests: (1) Brinell (HBW: 10 mm tungsten carbide ball under 3000 kg load, measuring surface indentation diameter $d$), (2) Rockwell (HRC: $120^\\circ$ diamond cone indenter under 150 kg major load, measuring depth of residual penetration $h$), (3) Vickers (HV: square-based diamond pyramid indenter), and (4) Knoop (HK: elongated micro-indentation for brittle materials and thin coatings).",
                    "examTip": "Found in Materials Science & Mechanical Design. Essential empirical NCEES rule for carbon steels: Ultimate Tensile Strength $S_{ut} \\approx 3.45 \\times \\text{HB}$ in $\\text{MPa}$ (or $500 \\times \\text{HB}$ in $\\text{psi}$). High hardness directly corresponds to high tensile yield strength and wear resistance, but decreased ductility and fracture toughness.",
