                        "question": "<p>A carbon steel machine component is tested with a standard Brinell hardness test using a $10\\text{ mm}$ tungsten carbide ball and yields a Brinell Hardness Number $\\text{HBW} = 220$.</p>\n<p>Using the empirical relationship in the NCEES FE Reference Handbook, the estimated ultimate tensile strength $S_{ut}$ of the steel in MPa is most nearly:</p>\n<div class=\"grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 my-2 font-mono text-xs\">\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(A) $450\\text{ MPa}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(B) $620\\text{ MPa}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(C) $760\\text{ MPa}$</div>\n  <div class=\"p-2 bg-slate-900/80 rounded border border-slate-700/60\">(D) $1100\\text{ MPa}$</div>\n</div>",
                        "solution": "<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 1: Identify NCEES Empirical Tensile Strength Formula</strong></p>\n<p>For carbon steels, the NCEES FE Handbook provides the empirical relation between Ultimate Tensile Strength $S_{ut}$ and Brinell hardness $\\text{HB}$:</p>\n$$S_{ut} \\approx 3.45 \\times \\text{HB} \\ [\\text{MPa}]$$\n<p><strong class=\"text-emerald-400\">Step 2: Calculate $S_{ut}$</strong></p>\n$$S_{ut} = 3.45 \\times 220 = 759\\text{ MPa} \\approx 760\\text{ MPa}$$\n<p class=\"mt-2 pt-2 border-t border-emerald-500/20\"><strong class=\"text-cyan-300\">Correct Answer:</strong> <span class=\"text-emerald-300 font-bold\">(C) $760\\text{ MPa}$</span></p>\n<p class=\"text-[11px] text-slate-400\"><em>FE Exam Tip:</em> In US Customary units: $S_{ut} \\approx 500 \\times \\text{HB}$ (in $\\text{psi}$). In SI units: $S_{ut} \\approx 3.45 \\times \\text{HB}$ (in $\\text{MPa}$).</p>"
