                    "title": "Properties of Normal Distribution",
                    "formula": "$$f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{(x - \\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}}; \\quad \\mu = \\text{Mean} = \\text{Median} = \\text{Mode}, \\quad \\sigma^2 = \\text{Variance};$$ $$\\text{Empirical Rule: } P(\\mu \\pm 1\\sigma) \\approx 68.27\\%, \\quad P(\\mu \\pm 2\\sigma) \\approx 95.45\\%, \\quad P(\\mu \\pm 3\\sigma) \\approx 99.73\\%$$",
                    "description": "The normal (Gaussian) distribution is a continuous, symmetric bell-shaped probability density function fully defined by its mean $\\mu$ (location parameter) and standard deviation $\\sigma$ (scale/dispersion parameter). Its total area under the curve is exactly 1 ($\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x)dx = 1$). Key properties include: (1) perfect symmetry about the mean ($\\text{Skewness} = 0$, $\\text{Kurtosis} = 3$), (2) mean, median, and mode coincide at the central peak $x = \\mu$, (3) inflection points occur symmetrically at $x = \\mu \\pm \\sigma$, and (4) the empirical 68–95–99.7% rule governs probabilities within 1, 2, and 3 standard deviations of the mean.",
                    "examTip": "Found in Probability and Statistics. Remember the empirical rule for quick FE mental math: $\\pm 1\\sigma \\approx 68.3\\%$, $\\pm 2\\sigma \\approx 95.4\\%$, $\\pm 3\\sigma \\approx 99.7\\%$. Because the curve is symmetric, the upper-tail probability beyond $\\mu + 2\\sigma$ is $(100\\% - 95.45\\%)/2 \\approx 2.28\\%$. Standard normal $Z$ transforms any normal distribution via $Z = (X - \\mu)/\\sigma$ with $\\mu_Z = 0$ and $\\sigma_Z = 1$.",
